# Cloudflare Workers Assets header rules. Copied into build/client/ by Vite,
# because `public/` is the publicDir, and read by the assets runtime.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Workers Assets defaults every asset to
# `cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`. MEASURED on production
# 2026-08-16, on one full document load of /admin/media: 14 asset requests, all
# 14 going to the network, every one returning headers only (transferSize 300
# against encoded bodies of up to 60 kB, so a 304 with the body already on
# disk). Median 689ms each and 1204ms for the slowest, against a document whose
# own response finished at 3146ms. The revalidations, not the HTML, are what
# stood between responseEnd and interactive.
#
# ONLY `/assets/*`, and the narrowness is the whole point. Everything under it
# is Vite build output whose filename CONTAINS its content hash, so the URL
# changes whenever the bytes do and a year is safe by construction.
#
# Nothing else gets this. The logo, `favicon.ico` and the icon suite sit at
# stable paths with no hash, so an immutable year would make replacing one
# unshippable, and this repo has already recorded browsers holding a stale
# favicon hard enough to look like a failed deploy.
#
# This changes STATIC ASSET caching only. HTML cache policy is untouched: it is
# set per route in the app and governed by the cache-header rule.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The markdown twin of every paper, written into public/research/publications/ by
# `build:publication-twins`. An ASSET rather than a route, so it is the one
# machine-readable surface on this site whose headers cannot be set in code:
# the Worker is never invoked for it. The grounds for that trade, and the
# measurement that proved an asset wins over the route matching the same path,
# are on app/lib/publications/twin.mjs.
#
# The two headers below are what a route would have set.
#
# CACHE-CONTROL matches SHARED_CACHE_CONTROL, the value every other public
# document on this site carries. The default for an asset is
# `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`, which is a round trip per fetch for a
# file that changes only on deploy.
#
# X-ROBOTS-TAG: NOINDEX, which the blog twin does NOT carry, and the difference
# is deliberate. A post's twin is the same prose as its page and there is no
# competition to lose. A paper page is a Scholar submission: it carries the
# citation_* tags, the JSON-LD and the canonical, and a second indexable URL
# holding the same title and abstract is the duplicate that costs a paper its
# ranking on the page that was built for it. The twin is for agents, and
# llms.txt is where they are told about it. Same treatment as llms.txt itself.
#
# LINK: REL="CANONICAL" names the paper page the twin stands for, as every
# markdown twin and llms file on this site does (app/lib/markdown-twin.ts,
# canonicalLink). `:splat` is the slug the `*` matched, and the page takes the
# trailing slash. Relative, which RFC 8288 resolves against the request URL, so
# the rule holds on workers.dev now and on the apex after the cutover without
# naming either.
/research/publications/*.md
  Cache-Control: public, s-maxage=600, stale-while-revalidate=86400
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
  Link: </research/publications/:splat/>; rel="canonical"

# Research and teaching twins, one file per page, written by build:content next to the HTML URL.
# Explicit paths, not a splat: a greedy /research/*.md would also match the paper twins above.
# noindex so the HTML page is the one that ranks. The link is relative, like the paper rule.
